# Master Profile — {Your Name}

> Single source of truth for every tailored resume, cover letter, and recruiter pitch the skill generates. Edit this file as your career evolves; the skill reads it on every run. Anything not in here will not appear on outputs.

## Identity

- **Headline candidates:** the LinkedIn-style title you'd want on each resume variant. List 3-6 options so the tailor can pick the closest match per JD. Don't invent new titles at tailoring time.
  - e.g. `Director of Software Development & Solution Architect`
  - e.g. `Engineering Manager`
  - e.g. `Principal Architect`
- **Location / phone / email / LinkedIn:** what goes on every cover letter and resume.
- **Self-identification:** the one-sentence elevator-pitch "who I am right now."

## Anonymization Rule

> The skill enforces this rule at build time via `tailor_config.json`. Document the prose version here so the LLM doing the tailoring understands *why* certain names get rewritten.

Never name `{Sensitive Employer}` on outputs. Use `{Anonymized Framing}` instead. Inversion: if the JD you're tailoring against is from `{Sensitive Employer}` itself, naming them is correct.

## Work history

> List every role you've ever held. The skill consolidates multi-role same-company entries at tailoring time (single entry, senior title, full date span, merged bullets).

### {Current Employer} — {Senior Title}
- **Dates:** {start} – Present
- **Location:** {city}
- **Engagement / context:** one-line description of the work.
- **Bullets:** list every achievement that could possibly be relevant to any future JD. The tailor will select the subset that matches each role. Lead each bullet with quantified impact when available.
- **Sub-roles** (junior → senior): list intermediate titles + date spans for the tailor to consolidate.

### {Previous Employer} — {Title}
- (same shape as above)

### {Older Employer, default-off}
- **Default visibility:** off on resume, available for cover-letter context.
- (rest same as above)

## Education

- `{Degree, School, Dates, GPA / honors if notable}` — list each on its own line. Always-include vs case-by-case is the tailor's call based on JD.

## Certifications

- `{Cert name — issuer — dates}` — list every active and lapsed credential. The tailor leads with whichever match the JD.

## Skills inventory

Group skills semantically, not chronologically. The tailor reorders groups so JD-required skills lead.

- **Cloud & Infrastructure:** ...
- **Languages:** ...
- **Methodologies:** ...

## Tailoring Rules

> Your personal tactical policy. The skill reads these at tailoring time and applies them. Without this section the skill will pause and ask you on every content decision — fill it in so the skill can act without interrupting. This is where you encode "always include X", "default-off Y", "lead with Z for FinTech roles" — anything specific to your career.

- **Work-history scope:** which employers always appear on the resume, which are default-off, which can appear in cover-letter prose only.
- **Bullet counts per entry:** e.g. "current role: 6-9 bullets; previous senior role: 4-6; older roles when included: 1-2 lines max."
- **Headline-to-archetype mapping:** map JD archetypes to your headline candidates (e.g. *"Director of Engineering JDs → lead with X headline"*).
- **Length defaults:** when to ship 1 page vs 2 pages (e.g. *"Director / VP / Architect roles → 2 pages; IC / Specialist roles → 1 page"*).
- **Trim order when overflowing the page cap:** which bullets to cut first (e.g. *"trim lowest-impact older-employer bullets first, then current-employer bullets, then shorten the longest lines"*).
- **Multi-role consolidation:** if you held promotions inside one employer, how to present them (e.g. *"single entry, senior title, full date span, merged bullets"*).
- **Education priority:** which education entries are always included, which are case-by-case.
- **Cover-letter differentiators:** the one-line differentiator pulled in by JD archetype (e.g. *"FinTech JDs → mention MS FinTech"*).
- **JD-archetype-to-emphasis mappings:** for each archetype you commonly see, the projects / employers / experiences to lead with and to de-emphasize.

